1. Problem Definition: Develop an AI system to predict the risk of patient readmission within 30 days after hospital discharge.

2. Objectives:
Identify patients at high risk of readmission to enable early intervention.
Reduce hospital readmission rates and associated costs.
Improve patient outcomes and care quality.

Stakeholders:
Patients
Healthcare providers (doctors, nurses, care coordinators)
Hospital administration
Data scientists and AI developers
Insurance companies
Data Strategy

Data Sources:
Electronic Health Records (EHRs) including medical history, diagnoses, treatments, lab results.
Patient demographics (age, gender, ethnicity).
Admission and discharge summaries.
Medication records.
Previous readmission records.
Social determinants of health (e.g., living conditions, support systems).

Ethical Concerns:
Patient privacy and data security: Ensuring compliance with HIPAA and protecting sensitive health information.
Bias and fairness: Avoiding model bias that could lead to unequal treatment of different patient groups.

Preprocessing Pipeline:
Data cleaning: Handle missing values, correct errors, and remove duplicates.
Data normalization and scaling for numerical features.
Encoding categorical variables (e.g., one-hot encoding for diagnosis codes).

Feature engineering:
Create features such as number of previous admissions, length of stay, comorbidity indices.
Aggregate lab results over time windows.
Extract temporal patterns from admission/discharge dates.
Data splitting into training, validation, and test sets.
Model Development

Model Selection:
Gradient Boosting Machines (e.g., XGBoost) due to their strong performance on tabular healthcare data and ability to handle heterogeneous features.

